<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego Gelmírez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1069–1140</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego Gelmírez was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and from 1120 the first archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, and the most powerful churchman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. He rebuilt the cathedral, promoted the pilgrimage to the tomb of Saint James and took a leading part in the politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Queen Urraca</a>'s reign and the upbringing of Alfonso VII. The Historia Compostellana records his career.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-gelmirez.jpg" alt="Diego Gelmírez" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfons7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diego Gelmírez, also written Xelmírez, was probably born at Catoira, where his father Gelmiro was custodian of the castle. He was educated at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>, king of León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1092 Raymond, count of Galicia, made him his notary and secretary, and in 1093 he administered the church of Compostela. After the death in 1095 of Dalmatius, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Compostela, the people of the see asked the king to name Diego administrator again during the vacancy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The pope authorised a new election in 1099, and Diego was elected in 1100, anointed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> 1101 and granted a pallium by Pope Paschal II the following year, though his see was not yet a metropolitan one. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> gave him secular rule of the city, and he worked to make Compostela a major place of pilgrimage. In 1120 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-ii">Pope Callixtus II</a> raised the see to an archbishopric and appointed him papal legate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>'s civil wars he changed sides more than once. Late in 1109 or early in 1110 he took the lead, with the knight Arias Pérez, of a brotherhood opposed to Pedro Fróilaz de Traba, guardian of the heir Alfonso Raimúndez. When he negotiated the surrender of Castrelo de Miño in 1110, Arias had him arrested with Pedro and Alfonso. Released, Diego joined the separatists and in 1111 crowned Alfonso Raimúndez King of Galicia in opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Queen Urraca</a> and her husband Alfonso the Battler.</p><p>Urraca stripped him of his secular authority late in 1113 at the demand of townspeople seeking communal rights, but restored it within a year. In 1120, according to the Historia Compostelana, she ordered the leading men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> to do homage to him as their lord, yet in the summer of 1121 she had him arrested at Castrelo; popular support forced his release. That year he razed an illegal castle built by Munio Peláez near Compostela. In 1126, after Urraca's death, he put down Arias's rebellion, besieging him at Lobeiro and Tabeirós until he surrendered. The Historia Compostelana, covering his episcopacy from 1100 to 1139, records these quarrels as a kind of gesta of his life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego Gelmírez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Way of St. James drew pilgrims from all over Europe, and <b>Diego Gelmírez</b> won the elevation of Compostela into an archdiocese in 1120, with jurisdiction over the old see of Mérida; the rivalry with Braga that followed made Braga the centre of the movement for Portugal's independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, promoted by the Leonese kings and by Archbishop <b>Diego Gelmírez</b>, became one of the great routes of Christendom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Galician high nobility, led by Pedro Fróilaz de Traba, crowned the child Alfonso VII king of Galicia at Santiago de Compostela, and Bishop <b>Diego Gelmírez</b> anointed him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-17">17 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
